The nightly enricher: 100,000 calls, 8 hours to make them

  counting 6,400 tokens a call (max output 400)
    312 calls a minute, limited by tokens; 10.4 workers use all of it
    5.3 hours to finish: fits in 8, +33% margin

  counting 10,000 tokens a call (max output 4,000)
    200 calls a minute, limited by tokens; 6.7 workers use all of it
    8.3 hours to finish: does not fit in 8, -4% margin

  Workers beyond that number do not finish sooner. They collect 429s, and their
  retries spend the limit that the workers doing real work needed.

Meeting summaries: 400,000 calls a day, arriving unevenly

                              calls/min   tokens/min  x the limit
  average minute                    278    3,500,000         1.8x
  busiest hour                      833   10,500,000         5.2x
  the limit admits                  159    2,000,000

  During the busiest hour the queue grows by 40,476 calls. Even the
  average minute needs 1.8x the limit, so the queue never drains:
  smoothing arrivals cannot help when the average itself is over. The
  design needs about 6x this limit at the busiest hour: a higher tier, capacity
  reserved with the provider, or a batch path with its own quota for summaries
  nobody reads within the hour — decided now, not discovered on launch day.
